# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/007_promotion_in_expressions.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 007_promotion_in_expressions
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Bitfield promotion as an operand, contrasting sub-int widths with a full 32-bit field
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
A bitfield narrower than int promotes to int even when its base type is unsigned, so
subtracting below zero yields a negative int; a 32-bit unsigned field promotes to unsigned
int and wraps modularly.  Both behaviours are defined, and that contrast is the subject of
the file.  Every product and shift is chosen to stay inside the range of the promoted type,
so no signed overflow occurs and every shift count is in range.
Every property is computed twice, once from literal constants and once from
volatile-qualified storage, so the constant folder's answer cannot stand in for the
one the backend computes at run time.
No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
